[Chapter "Exchange Variation"]
[Line "Rubinstein Variation (4.Bd3, 6.Bf4)"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation, Rubinstein Variation"]

{White releases the tension early. The position is symmetrical and quiet; good development decides it.} 1. e4 c6 {Prepares ...d5, so Black can challenge the centre with a pawn while the c8 bishop keeps its diagonal open.} 2. d4 d5 {The point of the Caro-Kann: hit e4 straight away, backed up by the c6 pawn.} 3. exd5 cxd5 {Recapture with the c-pawn: Black gets a half-open c-file and a symmetrical centre.} 4. Bd3 Nc6 {Develop and put pressure on d4.} 5. c3 Nf6 {Develop and guard d5.} 6. Bf4 Bg4 {Get the light-squared bishop out before ...e6 shuts it in.} 7. Qb3 {White hits b7.} 7... Qd7 {Guards b7 calmly and keeps the bishop on g4 supported.} 8. Nd2 e6 {Now close the centre; the bishop is already outside the pawn chain.} 9. Ngf3 Bd6 {Offer to trade the dark-squared bishops. The f4 bishop is one of White's best pieces.} 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 {Black is comfortable. A typical plan is ...b5–b4 against White's queenside pawns (the minority attack).} *
